> 
> I am trying to port a homemade library from S-PLUS 3.4 to 5.1.  I got
> the main function and the support functions running quite easily.  The
> main function returns objects of a certain old-style class.  Most of
> the methods I've written for this class work, but for 'lines' and
> 'points' the right function are not selected (I think S-PLUS tries to
> use the default method).  
> 
> The problem seems to originate from the difference in the definitions
> of e.g. 'plot' and 'lines':
> 
> > plot  # Method selection works
> function(x, ...)
> UseMethod("plot")

That is not the definition in use, which is certainly confusing. You need
to use selectMethod to see what plot does as a new-style generic.

> > lines  # Method selection fails
> function(x, y, ...)
> .Call("S_c_use_method", "lines")
> 
> What is the reason for this difference?  
> 
> Is it safe to change the definition of 'lines' and 'points' with
> 'setGeneric'?

No! First, you don't own them and second it would have widespread
consequences.

I don't fully understand your example, but the recommended way is to set a
method (new-style) for xyCall, which will then be used by lines (and plot
unless you have a more specific one). I think that is the key to getting
lines to work.

