Hello,

I have data which I try to analyse via a Cox model with a time by covariate
interaction.
In order to run the analysis, I need to split up the time scale, creating
times Tstart and Tstop. 
Then the following model is fitted:

coxph(formula = Surv(Tstart, Tstop, stat) ~ cd4 + cd4:I(Tstop - 5), data =
data),

However, depending on how the time dependency has been incorporated in the
data set, I get completely different results.
I can create time intervals running from one event time to the next one, or
I can create time intervals with fixed time span.
In the two data sets included at the end of this message, these approaches
have been used. 
I have deleted the lines that do not contribute to the likelihood.

The following results are obtained (S-PLUS 2000, release 2):

> coxph(Surv(Tstart , Tstop , stat) ~ cd4 + cd4:I(Tstop-5)  , data =
data1,na.action=na.omit)
Call:
coxph(formula = Surv(Tstart, Tstop, stat) ~ cd4 + cd4:I(Tstop - 5), data =
data1, na.action = na.omit)

                   coef exp(coef) se(coef)     z    p 
             cd4  12.69 3.23e+005    10.71  1.18 0.24
cd4:I(Tstop - 5)  -5.23 5.34e-003     3.37 -1.55 0.12

Likelihood ratio test=15.8  on 2 df, p=0.000381  n= 40 
> coxph(Surv(Tstart , Tstop , stat) ~ cd4 + cd4:I(Tstop-5)  , data =
data2,na.action=na.omit)
Call:
coxph(formula = Surv(Tstart, Tstop, stat) ~ cd4 + cd4:I(Tstop - 5), data =
data2, na.action = na.omit)

                   coef exp(coef) se(coef)       z    p 
